1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:07,693
Eleven thousand six hundred years ago, hunters with stone tools raised a ring of pillars on this hill.

2
00:00:07,693 --> 00:00:08,342
No farms.

3
00:00:08,342 --> 00:00:09,136
No pottery.

4
00:00:09,136 --> 00:00:09,785
No metal.

5
00:00:09,785 --> 00:00:12,600
And then, deliberately, they buried it.

6
00:00:13,066 --> 00:00:15,627
Each pillar weighs up to fifteen tons.

7
00:00:15,627 --> 00:00:22,500
They were carved six thousand years before Stonehenge, by people the textbooks said could not do this.

8
00:00:22,966 --> 00:00:24,118
So who were they?

9
00:00:24,118 --> 00:00:26,489
Did a lost civilization teach them?

10
00:00:26,489 --> 00:00:28,250
Did visitors from the sky?

11
00:00:28,250 --> 00:00:31,366
Or is the truth stranger, because it is human?

12
00:00:31,833 --> 00:00:34,300
Tonight we read it as a dig diary.

13
00:00:34,300 --> 00:00:35,824
One season at a time.

14
00:00:35,824 --> 00:00:38,799
And at the end, every claim gets a score.

15
00:00:39,266 --> 00:00:41,378
Facts first, theories labelled.

16
00:00:41,378 --> 00:00:42,399
Open the diary.

17
00:00:46,866 --> 00:00:47,779
Chapter one.

18
00:00:47,779 --> 00:00:48,463
The hill.

19
00:00:48,463 --> 00:00:50,061
Nineteen sixty-three.

20
00:00:50,061 --> 00:00:56,754
An American and Turkish survey team notes a hill near Sanliurfa covered in broken flint.

21
00:00:56,754 --> 00:01:00,633
They decide it is a medieval cemetery, and move on.

22
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:03,389
The flint is the clue they miss.

23
00:01:03,389 --> 00:01:09,257
Thousands of tiny blades and scrapers, thousands of years older than any cemetery.

24
00:01:09,257 --> 00:01:11,833
The hill is quiet for three decades.

25
00:01:12,299 --> 00:01:13,818
Nineteen ninety-four.

26
00:01:13,818 --> 00:01:19,024
German archaeologist Klaus Schmidt is searching for sites in the region.

27
00:01:19,024 --> 00:01:26,833
He reads the old survey, drives out, and sees a limestone slab the size of a table sticking from the ground.

28
00:01:27,299 --> 00:01:29,366
Most people see a natural rock.

29
00:01:29,366 --> 00:01:31,566
Schmidt sees the top of a pillar.

30
00:01:31,566 --> 00:01:36,833
He later said he realised within minutes that this was something extraordinary.

31
00:01:37,299 --> 00:01:38,903
Nineteen ninety-five.

32
00:01:38,903 --> 00:01:40,277
Excavations begin.

33
00:01:40,277 --> 00:01:43,561
Schmidt expects a little Neolithic village.

34
00:01:43,561 --> 00:01:50,433
Instead, the first trench reveals the heads of giant T-shaped pillars, packed in a circle.

35
00:01:50,900 --> 00:01:53,081
Radiocarbon dates come back.

36
00:01:53,081 --> 00:01:56,042
Around nine thousand five hundred BCE.

37
00:01:56,042 --> 00:01:58,692
That is the Pre-Pottery Neolithic.

38
00:01:58,692 --> 00:02:02,900
Humans were not supposed to be building monuments yet.

39
00:02:03,366 --> 00:02:08,098
The dates come from charcoal and other organic material in the fill.

40
00:02:08,098 --> 00:02:16,099
Later studies keep refining them, but the picture holds: this is the oldest known monumental architecture on Earth.

41
00:02:16,566 --> 00:02:17,427
Diary entry.

42
00:02:17,427 --> 00:02:19,147
The hill is not natural.

43
00:02:19,147 --> 00:02:27,966
It is a tell, an artificial mound, made of the rubble of the enclosures themselves, rebuilt again and again over centuries.

44
00:02:28,433 --> 00:02:32,432
Over the following years the team finds more circles.

45
00:02:32,432 --> 00:02:34,319
Enclosures A, B, C and D.

46
00:02:34,319 --> 00:02:42,166
Each one is a ring of pillars joined by low stone walls, with two taller pillars standing in the middle.

47
00:02:42,633 --> 00:02:43,504
Chapter two.

48
00:02:43,504 --> 00:02:44,376
The pillars.

49
00:02:44,376 --> 00:02:45,247
Each is a T.

50
00:02:45,247 --> 00:02:50,766
Some scholars see the T as a head with a flat top, and the pillar as a body.

51
00:02:51,233 --> 00:02:54,131
Look closer and the pillars wear things.

52
00:02:54,131 --> 00:02:56,595
Carved arms reach round the sides.

53
00:02:56,595 --> 00:02:58,479
Hands meet over the belly.

54
00:02:58,479 --> 00:03:02,392
Belts and loincloths of fox pelt are carved in relief.

55
00:03:02,392 --> 00:03:03,986
These are not columns.

56
00:03:03,986 --> 00:03:06,233
They are people, made of stone.

57
00:03:06,699 --> 00:03:08,042
How were they moved?

58
00:03:08,042 --> 00:03:16,300
The quarries are only a few hundred metres away, and an unfinished pillar still lies in the rock, nearly seven metres long.

59
00:03:16,766 --> 00:03:20,470
That unfinished giant weighs an estimated fifty tons.

60
00:03:20,470 --> 00:03:23,405
Nobody knows how they would have moved it.

61
00:03:23,405 --> 00:03:28,366
But the quarry itself, with its tool marks, proves they cut it by hand.

62
00:03:28,833 --> 00:03:32,784
The tools are flint chisels and hard stone pounders.

63
00:03:32,784 --> 00:03:33,468
No metal.

64
00:03:33,468 --> 00:03:38,255
Cutting limestone is not hard, because fresh limestone is soft.

65
00:03:38,255 --> 00:03:41,066
The work is huge, but not impossible.

66
00:03:41,533 --> 00:03:48,080
Experimental teams have moved multi-ton blocks with ropes, wooden sledges and a few dozen people.

67
00:03:48,080 --> 00:03:52,400
A work force of hundreds could raise a whole circle in a season.

68
00:03:52,866 --> 00:03:53,841
Chapter three.

69
00:03:53,841 --> 00:03:58,783
The two central pillars of each enclosure face the same way, southeast.

70
00:03:58,783 --> 00:04:02,194
Some researchers argue the layout tracks the sky.

71
00:04:02,194 --> 00:04:04,699
That is a theory, and a debated one.

72
00:04:05,166 --> 00:04:11,479
Claims of precise astronomical alignment depend on which pillars you count and when they stood.

73
00:04:11,479 --> 00:04:15,333
Many archaeologists say the evidence is not strong enough.

74
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:16,771
Chapter four.

75
00:04:16,771 --> 00:04:17,368
The zoo.

76
00:04:17,368 --> 00:04:19,759
The carvings are the real shock.

77
00:04:19,759 --> 00:04:26,333
Foxes, boars, snakes, scorpions, wild cattle, cranes and vultures, crowding the pillars.

78
00:04:26,800 --> 00:04:29,213
Almost all of them are dangerous.

79
00:04:29,213 --> 00:04:31,700
Not food, but predators and venom.

80
00:04:31,700 --> 00:04:34,333
It is a world of fear, carved large.

81
00:04:34,800 --> 00:04:42,317
At Enclosure D, Pillar forty-three, nicknamed the Vulture Stone, shows a vulture, a scorpion and a disc.

82
00:04:42,317 --> 00:04:47,233
People have read it as a funeral scene, a calendar, even a star map.

83
00:04:47,699 --> 00:04:49,534
Here is the careful reading.

84
00:04:49,534 --> 00:04:52,090
Some of the symbols may be pictographs.

85
00:04:52,090 --> 00:04:59,166
But there is no evidence of writing, and nothing like a proper script exists for another six thousand years.

86
00:04:59,633 --> 00:05:02,195
So what happened inside these rings?

87
00:05:02,195 --> 00:05:07,959
There are no graves, no houses in the enclosures, no temple floor with offerings.

88
00:05:07,959 --> 00:05:12,300
Archaeologists call it a gathering place, ritual or feasting.

89
00:05:12,766 --> 00:05:14,650
And the bones are telling.

90
00:05:14,650 --> 00:05:20,013
Tens of thousands of gazelle, wild cattle, and boar bones, butchered here.

91
00:05:20,013 --> 00:05:22,188
Feasting was a major activity.

92
00:05:22,188 --> 00:05:24,000
Big meals for big crowds.

93
00:05:24,466 --> 00:05:29,670
Giant stone vats were found too, which may have held a fermented drink.

94
00:05:29,670 --> 00:05:33,042
Some researchers think beer came before bread.

95
00:05:33,042 --> 00:05:37,000
It is a fun idea, supported only by indirect evidence.

96
00:05:37,466 --> 00:05:44,845
It fits a simple idea: a community of hunters, meeting across the region, building a monument together.

97
00:05:44,845 --> 00:05:49,000
And that idea, in turn, changes the story of civilisation.

98
00:05:49,466 --> 00:05:50,371
Chapter five.

99
00:05:50,371 --> 00:05:51,137
The burial.

100
00:05:51,137 --> 00:05:57,195
A twenty seventeen study reports skull fragments with carved grooves and drilled holes.

101
00:05:57,195 --> 00:06:02,000
Evidence of a skull cult, in which heads were prepared and displayed.

102
00:06:02,466 --> 00:06:04,276
The marks are cut with flint.

103
00:06:04,276 --> 00:06:05,587
They look deliberate.

104
00:06:05,587 --> 00:06:11,266
It suggests people honoured the dead with the same attention as the animals on the pillars.

105
00:06:11,733 --> 00:06:15,869
And then, around eight thousand BCE, the site is closed.

106
00:06:15,869 --> 00:06:17,641
Not abandoned in a rush.

107
00:06:17,641 --> 00:06:18,454
Backfilled.

108
00:06:18,454 --> 00:06:23,033
Tons of rubble were carried in and poured over the enclosures.

109
00:06:23,500 --> 00:06:25,246
Why bury a masterpiece?

110
00:06:25,246 --> 00:06:26,992
Maybe to end its power.

111
00:06:26,992 --> 00:06:28,587
Maybe to preserve it.

112
00:06:28,587 --> 00:06:31,320
Maybe the community simply moved on.

113
00:06:31,320 --> 00:06:33,066
Honestly, no one knows.

114
00:06:33,533 --> 00:06:38,322
A geophysical survey shows at least twenty enclosures under the mound.

115
00:06:38,322 --> 00:06:42,633
And only about ten percent of the site has ever been excavated.

116
00:06:43,100 --> 00:06:43,972
Chapter six.

117
00:06:43,972 --> 00:06:44,845
The village.

118
00:06:44,845 --> 00:06:50,009
For years the story was simple: a temple with no town, built by nomads.

119
00:06:50,009 --> 00:06:51,899
Then the evidence changed.

120
00:06:52,366 --> 00:07:01,184
Findings published in twenty nineteen show houses, grinding stones, cereal processing and a water supply of rock-cut cisterns.

121
00:07:01,184 --> 00:07:04,333
People lived here, at least part of the time.

122
00:07:04,800 --> 00:07:10,753
Sickle flints and grinding stones mean wild grain was being harvested and milled.

123
00:07:10,753 --> 00:07:16,633
The old question, which came first, farming or temples, is now far more tangled.

124
00:07:17,100 --> 00:07:25,265
Genetic studies place the origin of domesticated einkorn wheat on mountains just thirty kilometres away.

125
00:07:25,265 --> 00:07:30,133
Göbekli Tepe sits at the birth of agriculture, not outside it.

126
00:07:30,600 --> 00:07:35,669
So the monument may have come with the first settled communities, not after them.

127
00:07:35,669 --> 00:07:40,425
Building together may even have pushed people to farm, to feed the builders.

128
00:07:40,425 --> 00:07:42,866
That is a hypothesis, and a clever one.

129
00:07:43,333 --> 00:07:44,400
Chapter seven.

130
00:07:44,400 --> 00:07:45,238
The claims.

131
00:07:45,238 --> 00:07:46,382
First, a comet.

132
00:07:46,382 --> 00:07:55,833
A twenty seventeen paper reads the Vulture Stone as a date marker for a comet strike, around ten thousand eight hundred BCE.

133
00:07:56,300 --> 00:08:03,972
The idea links to the Younger Dryas impact hypothesis, which says a cosmic impact triggered a cold snap.

134
00:08:03,972 --> 00:08:09,800
Most specialists dispute both the dating of the carvings and the impact itself.

135
00:08:10,266 --> 00:08:13,097
The Younger Dryas cold period is real.

136
00:08:13,097 --> 00:08:16,076
A comet as its cause is not established.

137
00:08:16,076 --> 00:08:21,066
And reading a carved animal as a calendar is a reading, not a fact.

138
00:08:21,533 --> 00:08:22,948
Second claim: aliens.

139
00:08:22,948 --> 00:08:28,606
Ancient astronaut shows say pillars like these must have come from beyond the Earth.

140
00:08:28,606 --> 00:08:31,166
They call it too advanced for hunters.

141
00:08:31,633 --> 00:08:34,048
But the evidence is on the ground.

142
00:08:34,048 --> 00:08:36,393
Quarries with unfinished pillars.

143
00:08:36,393 --> 00:08:37,245
Flint tools.

144
00:08:37,245 --> 00:08:38,027
Tool marks.

145
00:08:38,027 --> 00:08:39,803
Animal bones from feasts.

146
00:08:39,803 --> 00:08:42,574
Nothing here is out of reach of people.

147
00:08:42,574 --> 00:08:45,700
It was just out of reach of our assumptions.

148
00:08:46,166 --> 00:08:49,653
There is also a quiet insult in the alien claim.

149
00:08:49,653 --> 00:08:55,102
It says hunter-gatherers could not have planned, organised and carved this.

150
00:08:55,102 --> 00:08:57,500
The dig says they absolutely did.

151
00:08:57,966 --> 00:09:03,393
Third claim: a lost advanced civilization, that taught them and then vanished.

152
00:09:03,393 --> 00:09:07,290
This one is harder to test, but there is no trace of it.

153
00:09:07,290 --> 00:09:10,700
No tools, no remains, no trade goods, no writing.

154
00:09:11,166 --> 00:09:15,434
Archaeologists look for evidence of absence at every level.

155
00:09:15,434 --> 00:09:21,366
The layers below the oldest pillars show hunters, not engineers from a lost world.

156
00:09:21,833 --> 00:09:22,375
Debrief.

157
00:09:22,375 --> 00:09:24,000
Time to score the diary.

158
00:09:24,466 --> 00:09:28,684
Date about eleven thousand six hundred years old: evidence.

159
00:09:28,684 --> 00:09:32,688
Pillars carved with flint by hunter-gatherers: evidence.

160
00:09:32,688 --> 00:09:35,548
Quarry with unfinished pillar: evidence.

161
00:09:35,548 --> 00:09:37,692
Deliberate backfill: evidence.

162
00:09:37,692 --> 00:09:40,266
Houses and cisterns found: evidence.

163
00:09:40,733 --> 00:09:43,250
Astronomical alignment: partial.

164
00:09:43,250 --> 00:09:46,161
Younger Dryas comet marker: doubtful.

165
00:09:46,161 --> 00:09:48,600
Lost civilization: no evidence.

166
00:09:48,600 --> 00:09:50,566
Alien builders: debunked.

167
00:09:51,033 --> 00:09:52,056
So the verdict.

168
00:09:52,056 --> 00:09:55,669
Göbekli Tepe is real, it is ancient, and it is human.

169
00:09:55,669 --> 00:10:03,033
And that is the mystery that matters, because it forces us to rewrite when people started building together.

170
00:10:03,500 --> 00:10:06,127
Klaus Schmidt died in twenty fourteen.

171
00:10:06,127 --> 00:10:09,239
His team still digs, under Turkish direction.

172
00:10:09,239 --> 00:10:14,633
With ninety percent still buried, the biggest chapters may not be written yet.

173
00:10:15,100 --> 00:10:21,897
Nearby Karahan Tepe shows the same T-shaped pillars, carved human heads and a rock-cut hall.

174
00:10:21,897 --> 00:10:25,000
The network is bigger than anyone guessed.

175
00:10:25,466 --> 00:10:28,811
UNESCO listed Göbekli Tepe in twenty eighteen.

176
00:10:28,811 --> 00:10:35,500
Visitors can now walk above the pillars and see what the hunters built before anything else.

177
00:10:35,966 --> 00:10:44,705
Next case: Puma Punku, in the Andes, where stone blocks with razor straight angles have sparked claims of machined stone.

178
00:10:44,705 --> 00:10:47,666
Was it lost technology or patient masons?

179
00:10:48,133 --> 00:10:50,807
What do you think the hunters built this for?

180
00:10:50,807 --> 00:10:55,799
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
